  "query_preview": "(Today through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 327 AM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nDeep ridging over the central CONUS will be the predominant driver \nof our upcoming weather over the next several days. Warm SW flow has \npushed temperatures well above normal, with high temperatures in the \nlow to mid 90s through at least Thursday. This in combination with \ndew points in the low 70s will push heat index values above 100 each \nafternoon, prompting a Heat Advisories until 8PM Thursday.\n\nThe ridging is then expected to retrograde which could introduce \nshowers and storms into portions of the area as early as late in the \nday Thursday. However, there is a bit of uncertainty with this as it \nis unclear on how quickly the ridge will drift back west and it's \namplitude. Confidence in convection on the northern periphery of the \nridge is high, but how far south this area of convection reaches is \nwhere confidence wanes. Currently the expectation is for a majority \nof any precipitation Thursday afternoon to remain just north of the \narea, but the margin for error is small. Should these storms make it \ninto central Indiana, there is a threat of severe weather with them \nwith damaging winds the main threat. \n\nA strengthening trough over the east coast will help to push the \nridge further west late week into the weekend. This could lead to \nadditional chances for showers and storms but again low confidence \non timing and location. \n\nTemperatures will be slightly lower going into the weekend but still \nexpected to be above normal. The start of the new week could see \nnearer to normal temps but well above normal can't be ruled out for \nthe extended period with a few chances for PoPs.",
